#{[17592186113668 :answer-field/type #edb/ref 17592186045492] [17592186113668 :answer-field/id #uuid "6df614d1-97ad-5df6-9fe1-d9f076fa7b0a"] [17592186113681 :answer-field/type #edb/ref 17592186045492] [17592186113681 :answer-field/id #uuid "d6fbb24c-bc55-59f9-bde8-a6c719ed0e29"] [17592186115782 :question/problem "A body of mass $25kg$ is released from rest and falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the body's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\ge 0$ seconds after release, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the body is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the body’s velocity is $12m/s$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $7.4m/s^{2}$. Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8m/s^{2}$, and the only forces acting on the body are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t)$, expressing your answer in terms of $t$ **only.**\n\n$v(t) =$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186115783 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186113665] [17592186115783 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186363761] [17592186115785 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186113667] [17592186115785 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186363763] [17592186115789 :question/problem "A weather sensor of mass $30kg$ is launched downward with an initial velocity of $5m/s$ and continues to fall vertically. Let $v(t)$ be the sensor's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\ge 0$ seconds after launch, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the sensor is proportional to its velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the sensor’s velocity is $8m/s$ and its instantaneous acceleration is $8.2m/s^{2}$. Assume the acceleration due to gravity is $g = 9.8m/s^{2}$, and the only forces acting on the sensor are the weight due to gravity and drag.\n\nFind the equation for the velocity $v(t)$, expressing your answer in terms of $t$ **only.**\n\n$v(t) =$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186124937 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}.$"] [17592186124953 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (-2,3)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}=$"] [17592186124953 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-2,3)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-2,3)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2,3)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy} & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-2)^{2}+(-2)\\cdot {3}^{2}}{(-2)^{2}+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-18}{4-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10}{-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186125220 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-{y}^{2}z}.$"]}
